Z||000028||Wannabe||05-23-2001||04:16 PM||||If you REALLY want to keep it cheap and easy, why don't you just get a set of performance pads like KVR which you can order locally and only  cost about $50CDN per axle, some high perf brake fluid like Motul, and a set of SS brake lines. The pads will noticably improve your braking power and the fluid and lines will provide a  slight improvement in feel. There are other pads you can get that cost more than the KVRs, and will have more grip and less fade but will dust more. There are a few significant improvements you can make before making major brake upgrades. The biggest benefit of the GA upgrade though, is the cost of the rotors - $20 a piece from Craps! Calipers are very cheap too.||||reg||1
Z||000029||Jay||05-23-2001||04:28 PM||||Grinthock, I put Stainless steel braided lines with cross drilled rotors on my car.  It is alot better than stock.  I have a set of rear (only) cross drilled rotors that are already machined and ready to be installed.  I can't use them because of the Northstar axles in the back.  If you want them, make me an offer.  Because they are cross drilled they disappate the heat better.<P>------------------<BR>Jay<BR>1986 Northstar GT<BR>www.members.home.net/jaygrande||||reg||1
Z||000030||Haze_Performance||05-23-2001||04:53 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by grinthock:<BR><B>What other upgrades have people here done to their brakes?  I hear all these things I can do, what's practical?  What's been done?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I just replaced all my brake components. I used Earl's Stainless Lines, Fiero Store Crossdrilled Rotors, New Calipers, Heavy Duty Bearings, All new e-brake cables & a big bore Master Cylinder. <P>There was a HUGH difference in pedal feel & braking distance over the stock setup.<P>I did lots of research about brake upgrades for the Fiero. It comes down to, 96% of the Fieros out there don't need a huge rotor upgrade. Unless someone is using there car for heavy duty SCCA course driving, you don't need to waste your money. If its just for looks its a different story....<P>My 2 1/2 cents........  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  <BR><P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.ws6fiero.com" TARGET=_blank><BR>           <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/haze8588logo.gif">      </A>       <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/WS6_Fiero/photo.html" TARGET=_blank><BR>Pennock's Fiero Forum Members Photo Page</A><BR><I>If you want to add your Fiero to the collection, post or send me a photo. (Sent photos ONLY to: purplehazeracing@yahoo.com )</I><P><A HREF="http://www.clubs.yahoo.com/clubs/ws6fiero" TARGET=_blank>WS6 Fiero Club</A>||||reg||10
Z||000031||Slammed Fiero||05-23-2001||05:02 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1607505&a=12259132&p=49046355&Sequence=0&res=high"> <P>Go easy on those cross drilled rotors. This is from a street driven 911.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1607505&a=12259132&p=48230089&Sequence=0&res=high">     <BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals..Autocrossed , Tracked , abused.||||reg||1
Z||000032||Haze_Performance||05-23-2001||05:18 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR><B>  <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1607505&a=12259132&p=49046355&Sequence=0&res=high">  <P>Go easy on those cross drilled rotors. This is from a street driven 911.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>OUCH!!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.ws6fiero.com" TARGET=_blank><BR>           <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/haze8588logo.gif">      </A>       <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/WS6_Fiero/photo.html" TARGET=_blank><BR>Pennock's Fiero Forum Members Photo Page</A><BR><I>If you want to add your Fiero to the collection, post or send me a photo. (Sent photos ONLY to: purplehazeracing@yahoo.com )</I><P><A HREF="http://www.clubs.yahoo.com/clubs/ws6fiero" TARGET=_blank>WS6 Fiero Club</A>||||reg||11
Z||000033||grinthock||05-23-2001||08:59 PM||||From what I hear, the cross drilled rotors tend to crack when turned too much, I am getting NEW cross drilled rotors...  AND it's a deal, I am not a fan, BUT I am getting a deal...||||reg||1
Z||000034||KissMySSFiero||05-23-2001||10:33 PM||||i may have missed it.  but can this be used on an '88?<P>------------------<BR>JOEJOE Aol:SSFiero <P>88FORMULA<BR>      <A HREF="mailto:SSFiero@Aol.com">SSFiero@Aol.com</A>       <P>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FORUMSIG1.jpg">           <BR>"Kids in the backseat cause accidents,  accidents in the backseat caused me!!"||||reg||1
Z||000035||Slammed Fiero||05-23-2001||11:31 PM||||The cracks in that picture have nothing to do with turning the rotor, and usually the reason they crack isn't because of turning. Those were brand new on an occasionally driven 911. Driven hard mind you , but just street duty. The cracks are due to stress and heat disipation ,your decreasing surface area when you cross drill. When you get brake fade it's from an invisible gas building up between the rotor and the pad , the cross drilling is designed to vent this gas. Lots of guys now are running gas slotted rotors , You don't often see cross drilled rotors on high end sports cars anymore because they crack , they have become more of a fashion thing. Porsche I believe  does it with the ceramic option on the 911 Turbo , but thats a different issue as the properties of the ceramic material are different than that of the regular rotor. <P>On my 11.25" converison I had the rotors sent to <A HREF="http://www.frozenrotors.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.frozenrotors.com/</A> <BR>The company is called Diversified Cryogenics. They freeze the rotors , it changes the properties of the metal to a certian extent and helps to disapate the heat far better/ quicker.<P>Here's there price break down , not really useful for street duty cars, But nice for the more high performance / occasional track driven car<P>Rotor Treatment $56.00 each<BR>Gas Slotting $45.00 per rotor<BR>Slotting/Drilling $60.00 each<P>you can talk them down for a package deal , We sent my rotors in as well as our 911's<P>I doubt you will see any problems though since your using the cross drilled rotors on the rear , the majority of the braking on any mid / rear engine car is done by the front wheels.<BR><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1607505&a=12259132&p=48230089&Sequence=0&res=high">     <BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals..Autocrossed , Tracked , abused.||||reg||1
Z||000036||rogergarrison||05-23-2001||11:47 PM||||thanks for posting what i was thinking Jonathan. Ive heard a lot about crossdrilled rotor cracking from the holes. Mine are still stock, but when it need them Im upgrading the pads and hoses only. The brakes arent the best but they work. On the race car I had, we had the brakes very hard, so we wouldnt have wheel lockup under braking. Very ez to lock them and spin out of control, so im used to having to press hard to stop fast.||||reg||1
